[THOUGHT]  
The clinical picture remains consistent with a rapidly growing, 5-cm, non-tender, polylobulated, multinodular right breast mass in a patient with no risk factors or alarm symptoms. Key new information: **no spontaneous nipple discharge**, reinforcing that the lesion is likely not a classic central (subareolar) papilloma — which commonly presents with serosanguineous discharge — but rather a *peripheral* atypical papilloma or a more complex intraductal neoplasm (e.g., papillary DCIS or encapsulated papillary carcinoma).  

The CNB finding — *"papillary projections of epithelial-lined stroma with hyperplasia and atypia"* — is diagnostic of **atypical papilloma**, a well-recognized high-risk lesion. Critically, this diagnosis *cannot be definitively confirmed or ruled out as malignant* on CNB alone due to sampling limitations and overlapping features with low-grade papillary carcinomas. Distinction hinges on architectural and cytologic criteria (e.g., invasion, necrosis, mitotic activity, myoepithelial layer integrity), which require full excision and comprehensive histologic evaluation.  

Missing data — including IHC (p63/CD34 to assess myoepithelial continuity; Ki-67 for proliferation), axillary ultrasound (to exclude nodal involvement), MRI (to evaluate multifocality/disease extent), and CA 15-3 (not clinically indicated here but noted as unmeasured) — underscores that current workup is *incomplete*. However, **surgical excision remains the standard of care regardless**, per national guidelines (e.g., NCCN, ASCO), for any atypical papilloma diagnosed on core biopsy — primarily to exclude under-sampled malignancy and obtain definitive architecture.

[SUMMARY]  
**Final Integrated Clinical Assessment**  

- **Presentation**: 3-week history of rapidly enlarging, 5-cm, non-tender, polylobulated, multinodular mass in the outer quadrant of the right breast. No skin changes, lymphadenopathy, systemic symptoms, or nipple discharge. No personal/family history of hormone-sensitive cancers.  

- **Imaging**: Ultrasound shows a smooth but polylobulated mass — morphologically suspicious for intraductal papillary lesion (benign or malignant).  

- **Core Needle Biopsy Diagnosis**: **Atypical intraductal papilloma**, defined by papillary fronds with epithelial hyperplasia and cytologic atypia. This is a *high-risk benign lesion*, associated with:  
  • ~10–20% risk of upgrade to DCIS or invasive carcinoma on excision;  
  • Increased long-term risk of subsequent breast cancer (2–4× general population).  

- **Critical Diagnostic Gaps (Not Yet Assessed)**:  
  • Myoepithelial integrity (requires p63 and/or CD34 IHC);  
  • Proliferative index (Ki-67);  
  • Mitotic count (per 10 HPF);  
  • Axillary nodal status (axillary ultrasound pending);  
  • Multifocality/extent (breast MRI not performed);  
  • Serum tumor markers (CA 15-3 — not indicated in early-stage evaluation but documented as unmeasured).  

- **Urgent Next Step**: **Therapeutic and diagnostic surgical excision** (wide local excision or lumpectomy) with margin assessment. This is *not optional* — it is the only way to:  
  (a) Rule out invasive carcinoma or DCIS missed on CNB;  
  (b) Determine true size, architecture, and margins;  
  (c) Enable definitive classification (e.g., encapsulated papillary carcinoma vs. papillary DCIS vs. benign atypical papilloma);  
  (d) Guide adjuvant management (e.g., radiation, endocrine therapy, surveillance intensity).  

- **No role for immediate systemic staging (e.g., CT, bone scan, CA 15-3)** given absence of B-symptoms, normal physical exam, and localized presentation.  

**Conclusion**: This is a high-priority surgical case. Diagnosis of atypical papilloma on CNB mandates excision — irrespective of imaging or biomarker results — to prevent underdiagnosis of malignancy and inform risk-adapted management.

